Dear Krisztina,

No, "1" in linearComb does not mean that detectability is 1.

If you want to use backTransform and linearComb, then you try to understand what a design matrix is. A good introduction can be found here:
http://www.phidot.org/software/mark/docs/book/pdf/chap6.pdf

I think you can skip some of the algebra in this chapter but you should understand the design matrices (chapter 6.2). Then you will understand how backTransform and linearComb can be used.

Cheers, Beni

Gesendet: Montag, 13. Januar 2020 um 16:49 Uhr
Von: "Krisztina Csiki" <krisztin...@gmail.com>
An: unma...@googlegroups.com
Betreff: Re: [unmarked] Check if detection estimate is >0.15

I have checked the unmarked info document about the backTransform() function, but I still don't understand what the first entry in the "coefficients"-parentheses means. If I write 1, does it mean that detectability is set to 1? Or does it symbolize the null model for detectability?

Another question I have is if/how  I can get an estimate about detectability if I have a detectability model with a factor instead of a continuous variable. Is it even possible to backtranfsform?

